gnulib's getlocalename_l_unsafe() asks each C library for the name of a
locale object in its own private way -- glibc's struct, FreeBSD's
querylocale, Haiku's C++ backend -- and stops the build with #error on
any library it does not recognise. picolibc is one.

POSIX.1-2024 made this a standard function, getlocalename_l(), and
picolibc has it; configure already finds it (HAVE_GETLOCALENAME_L). Use
it when it is there, ahead of giving up.

--- a/lib/getlocalename_l-unsafe.c	2026-09-26 17:20:22.468402691 -0600
+++ b/lib/getlocalename_l-unsafe.c	2026-09-26 17:20:22.485565819 -0600
@@ -658,6 +658,10 @@
       };
       const char *name = ((struct __locale_t *) locale)->mb_cur_max == 4 ? "C.UTF-8" : "C";
       return (struct string_with_storage) { name, STORAGE_INDEFINITE };
+#elif HAVE_GETLOCALENAME_L
+      /* POSIX.1-2024's own answer, where the C library has it (picolibc
+         does). Its result lives as long as the locale object.  */
+      return (struct string_with_storage) { getlocalename_l (category, locale), STORAGE_OBJECT };
 #else
  #error "Please port gnulib getlocalename_l-unsafe.c to your platform! Report this to bug-gnulib."
 #endif
